  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1259 AM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nWeak high pressure between two lows to the southwest and northeast \ntoday. This is short lived as the next low makes a northeastwards \ntrek into the Dakotas. The systems loops from western Nebraska \nnortheast into northeast South Dakota and eastern North \nDakota/northwest Minnesota. This trajectory brings much of the CWA \nback into the warm sector. GFS/RRFS BUFKIT profiles support stratus \nto between 4-6kft thickness across the northeast, thick enough for \ndrizzle which shows up as low QPF in deterministic guidance.  850mb \ntemperatures are back up to between +6 and +8C. Mixing will be \nsomewhat lilted by warm advection and cloud cover and the 25th/75th \nrange in NBM temperatures is only about 4-6F...which is coincidentally \nthe greatest range in highs/lows we will see over the next few days. \n \nCold advection on the backside results in 850mb temperatures \ndropping to -2C to -5C with a ridge that extends down across the \nDakotas from a high moving from central Canada into the Western \nLakes region. The slow movement of this high pressure system which \nruns through the weekend into the start of next weeks \nmeans temperatures will be much closer to seasonal averages.\n\nNo systems of significance thereafter with a weak wave for the \nweekend. Profiles indicate some weak mid-level instability \nassociated with the cooler airmass aloft, but overall this will be \nreflected as mainly mild clouds. Another trough moves through the \nregion for the middle of next week. NBM probabilities of \nprecipitation type leaning heavily on snow over rainfall. Not much \nof a system overall, with the main forcing for precipitation \nassociated with an area of isentropic ascent. Still much time \nbetween now and then for models to hash out better details.",
